--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Scope: what the MIT License above covers, and what it does not
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The MIT License above applies to the source code of this repository only:
the application code, scripts, SQL migrations, tests and configuration.
It does not apply to the content and data described below.

1. Original learning content: CC BY-SA 4.0

   The learning content written for this project is licensed under the
   Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International licence
   (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/).
   Copyright (c) 2026 Denver.

   This covers the hand-written words, Arabic hints, example sentences,
   dictation sentences, messages, scenarios and spelling-rule texts in:

     - src/data/words/          (the word bank, one file per category)
     - src/data/rules.ts        (the spelling-rule explanations)
     - src/data/writing/        (dictation sentences, messages and scenarios)
     - scripts/content/hints/   (Arabic hints for the open-data library words)
     - scripts/content/original/ (original example sentences)

   and the same hints and examples where the build copies them into
   public/content/v1/. Code in these files (types, helpers) is under the
   MIT License above.

   Attribution: "Kitaba (https://kitaba-amber.vercel.app), CC BY-SA 4.0".

2. Third-party data and audio: their own licences

   Data and audio from other sources keep the licence they were published
   under. They are not covered by the MIT License or by the CC BY-SA 4.0
   grant above.

     - public/audio/v1/      Pronunciation recordings from Wikimedia Commons
                             (including Lingua Libre), modified, each under
                             the licence named on its row in the audio
                             credits file in that folder (mostly CC BY-SA
                             3.0 or 4.0; some CC BY or CC0), and generated
                             files from the public-domain Piper
                             en_US-ljspeech-high voice.
     - public/content/v1/    The open-data library. Tatoeba sentences and
                             translations are under CC BY 2.0 FR, with each
                             sentence's author named in the Tatoeba credits
                             file in that folder. Word data derived from
                             SCOWL, VarCon and the CMU Pronouncing Dictionary
                             keeps their notices.
     - public/dict/          (copied at build time from dictionary-en)
                             MIT and the SCOWL licence.

   The full list of sources, licences, authors and reproduced notices is
   on the credits page: https://kitaba-amber.vercel.app/credits
   and in scripts/content/sources.json.
